thread:
leo_customer: Hey team, streaming responses from the TS SDK in our Vercel edge function keep dropping midway. We get partial chunks then stream closes. Happens across multiple requests, around 30-60s in. No 5xx from Redwood, just `Unexpected end of stream` in browser console.

katie_support: Sorry you're hitting this — can you paste minimal repro and SDK version? Also, are you using the Node or Edge build of the SDK?

leo_customer: Repro below (Vercel Edge):
```ts
import { Redwood } from '@redwood/inference';
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.RW_API_KEY });
export default async (req) => {
  const res = await client.chat.stream({ model: 'gpt-open-3.1-edge', messages: [{ role: 'user', content: 'stream test' }] });
  const reader = res.body.getReader();
  while (true) {
    const { value, done } = await reader.read();
    if (done) break;
    // append to response
  }
  return new Response('ok');
};
```
Using `@redwood/inference` 0.5.1 (installed via npm).

sam_runtime: Quick thought — 0.5.x had a different streaming protocol and there were fixes in 0.6.0 around partial chunk buffering and SSE boundary handling for edge runtimes. Can you try upgrading to 0.6.2?

leo_customer: We pinned 0.5.1 because of another app. Upgrading might be hard right now. Is there a config/workaround?

katie_support: Two possible workarounds:
1) Switch to the SDK edge bundle explicitly (we shipped a separate ESM edge entry) — import from `@redwood/inference/edge` so runtime gets correct fetch/readable wrappers.
2) Add defensive retry/concat logic for missing chunk boundaries. e.g., accumulate partial UTF-8 chunks and only decode when you detect a full SSE delimiter (`

`).

alex_engineer: Also check if the Vercel edge worker is timing out or being torn down due to cold starts — edge has execution time limits. But since you said 30-60s, that could be a platform worker TTL. Can you share Vercel logs?

leo_customer: Vercel shows no function timeout. The browser shows `ERR_INCOMPLETE_CHUNKED_ENCODING` and in the SDK client we see `TypeError: stream closed`.

sam_runtime: Another common issue: API key rotation causing 401 mid-stream if you rotate keys and the client retries with a new key incorrectly. Not likely here but pls confirm your key is stable during test.

leo_customer: Key hasn't rotated. Billing is fine (no 402). We did notice sometimes small latency spikes before drop. Could be backpressure?

katie_support: Backpressure in edge contexts can cause the runtime to pause and the provider to decide to close idle connections. The SDK 0.6.2 added continuous read heartbeats for long streams to avoid that. Upgrading should be first try.

leo_customer: Ok will try upgrade in staging. If we can't upgrade, is there a recommended proxy pattern?

alex_engineer: You can proxy streaming through a serverful Node process (not edge) that keeps a stable connection to Redwood and re-streams to client. Node runtime uses native stream semantics so it's less fragile. But that's a heavier setup.

leo_customer: Got it. One more: our app uses `fetch` polyfills in the edge build — could that break chunked transfer?

sam_runtime: Yes — polyfills that buffer the whole response will break streaming. Make sure you're not using any fetch wrapper that drains the stream (e.g., calling `response.text()` in middleware).

katie_support: Action items for us: 1) we'll confirm the 0.6 stream fixes address SSE chunk boundary in edge. 2) if you can share a reduced repro repo we can run on Vercel. 3) we can also trace heartbeats and backpressure on our side if you grant a support trace token.

leo_customer: Sending a small repro repo link in DM and will try upgrade. Thanks. :thumbsup:

deploy-bot: FYI: we've just rolled SDK @redwood/inference v0.6.2 to npm (release notes: edge streaming fixes, SSE delimiter handling, heartbeat for long streams).

katie_support: Follow-up: if after upgrade you get 401 mid-stream or `INVALID_API_KEY` errors, let us know — that can indicate a proxy or rate-limit retry replacing auth header. We'll check logs for your thread ts if you send request ids.

leo_customer: Will do, thanks all.
